The Kore Agentic Framework Dashboard aims to empower users of all technical backgrounds to effectively build, monitor, and manage agentic pipelines. This dashboard will be the central hub for interacting with Kore.

Here's a detailed breakdown of key features, categorized for clarity:

---

## Kore Dashboard: Key Feature List

### I. Core Agent Management & Development

**1. Agent Registry/Catalog:**
    *   **Description:** A central repository showcasing all agents available within the Kore framework. Users can browse, search, and filter agents based on various criteria (e.g., domain, capabilities, creator, status). Each agent entry provides a detailed overview.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Agent Profile:** Name, description, version, creator, last updated, tags/keywords, associated skills/tools.
        *   **Capabilities Matrix:** A visual representation (e.g., a table or, even better, a tag cloud) outlining what the agent can *do* (e.g., perform web searches, write code, analyze data).
        *   **Dependencies:** List of tools, models, or other agents it relies on.
        *   **Deployment Status:** Indicates if the agent is active, idle, or in development.
        *   **Quick Actions:** Options to "View Details," "Edit," "Clone," "Deploy," "Delete."

**2. Agent Creation & Configuration Wizard:**
    *   **Description:** A guided, step-by-step interface for building new agents or modifying existing ones. This caters to both technical and non-technical users.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Name & Description:** Basic metadata.
        *   **LLM Selection:** Choose the underlying Large Language Model (e.g., GPT-4, Claude, custom local models) and configure parameters (temperature, max tokens, system prompt).
        *   **Tool/Skill Assignment:** Select from a library of pre-built tools (e.g., code interpreter, web scraper, database query tool) or define custom tools.
        *   **Memory Management:** Configure memory type (e.g., short-term, long-term, vector store), capacity, and retrieval strategies.
        *   **Prompt Engineering Interface:** A dedicated editor for crafting system prompts, few-shot examples, and general instruction sets. Includes version control for prompts.
        *   **Evaluation Metrics Configuration:** Define how the agent's performance will be measured.
        *   **Access Control:** Define who can use, modify, or view the agent.

**3. Tool/Skill Library & Management:**
    *   **Description:** A comprehensive catalog of all available tools and skills that agents can leverage. Users can browse, add, edit, and manage these capabilities.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Pre-built Tool Catalog:** Integrations with common services (e.g., Jira, Slack, GitHub, various APIs).
        *   **Custom Tool Definition:** Interface for defining custom Python functions or API endpoints as tools (code editor, schema definition).
        *   **Tool Versioning:** Track changes and ensure agents use compatible tool versions.
        *   **Tool Documentation:** Clear explanations and example usage for each tool.

**4. Prompt Engineering Studio (Advanced):**
    *   **Description:** A dedicated environment for experienced users to fine-tune prompts, explore different LLM behaviors, and test prompt variations.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Multi-LLM Comparison:** Run the same prompt against multiple LLMs and compare outputs side-by-side.
        *   **Prompt Versioning & History:** Track changes to prompts, allowing rollbacks and comparisons.
        *   **Evaluation Playground:** Integrate with defined evaluation metrics to test prompt effectiveness.
        *   **Token Counter & Cost Estimator:** Real-time feedback on prompt length and estimated cost.

### II. Pipeline Orchestration & Design

**1. Agentic Pipeline Builder (Visual & Code-based):**
    *   **Description:** A powerful interface for connecting multiple agents and tools into complex workflows. Supports both visual drag-and-drop design and code-based configuration.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Drag-and-Drop Canvas:** Visually connect agents, tools, and conditional logic.
        *   **Node Library:** Access to all registered agents, tools, and control flow nodes (e.g., Start, End, Branch, Loop, Parallel).
        *   **Input/Output Mapping:** Define how data flows between different nodes in the pipeline.
        *   **Conditional Logic & Branching:** Implement if/else statements and decision points.
        *   **Looping Constructs:** Specify iterations for repetitive tasks.
        *   **Error Handling & Retries:** Configure strategies for dealing with failures.
        *   **Pipeline Versioning:** Track changes to pipeline definitions.
        *   **Code View (YAML/JSON):** Ability to view and edit the underlying pipeline definition in a structured format for advanced users.

**2. Pipeline Execution & Testing:**
    *   **Description:** Tools for running pipelines, simulating inputs, and observing their behavior.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Manual Trigger:** Run a pipeline on demand with specified inputs.
        *   **Scheduled Runs:** Configure pipelines to run at specific intervals or times.
        *   **Sandbox Environment:** Test pipelines without affecting production systems.
        *   **Mock Inputs:** Simulate various input scenarios for thorough testing.
        *   **Step-by-Step Execution Trace:** Visualize the execution path of the pipeline, highlighting which agents were invoked and what data they processed.

### III. Monitoring & Analytics

**1. Real-time Agent/Pipeline Activity Feed:**
    *   **Description:** A live stream of events detailing agent and pipeline actions, useful for immediate oversight.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Event Log:** Timestamps, agent/pipeline ID, action taken (e.g., "Agent X started task Y," "Tool Z executed," "Pipeline A completed").
        *   **Search & Filter:** Quickly find specific events.
        *   **Severity Levels:** Differentiate between informational, warning, and error events.

**2. Performance & Usage Metrics Dashboard:**
    *   **Description:** Comprehensive dashboards to track the health, efficiency, and cost of agents and pipelines.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Agent/Pipeline Uptime & Availability:** Gauge reliability.
        *   **Execution Success Rate:** Percentage of successful runs vs. failures.
        *   **Average Execution Time:** Identify bottlenecks.
        *   **LLM Token Usage & Cost:** Detailed breakdown of token consumption and estimated spend per agent/pipeline.
        *   **Tool Usage Frequency:** Identify most/least utilized tools.
        *   **Error Rates (by type):** Categorize and track common errors.
        *   **Resource Utilization:** CPU, memory, and API call limits (if applicable).
        *   **Custom Metrics:** Ability to define and track domain-specific performance indicators.

**3. Health & Alerting System:**
    *   **Description:** Proactive notifications for critical events and performance deviations.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Configurable Alert Rules:** Set thresholds for metrics (e.g., "If error rate > 5% for Agent X, alert").
        *   **Notification Channels:** Email, Slack, Microsoft Teams, PagerDuty integration.
        *   **Incident Management Integration:** Link alerts to existing incident management systems.
        *   **Historical Alert Log:** Review past alerts and resolution statuses.

**4. Traceability & Debugging:**
    *   **Description:** Tools to understand the "thought process" of agents and pinpoint issues in workflows.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Agent Thought Process Viewer:** See the internal monologue and reasoning steps an agent took (e.g., "Planning phase," "Tool selection," "Input processing").
        *   **Input/Output Inspection:** View the exact inputs provided to an agent/tool and their corresponding outputs at each step.
        *   **Contextual Data View:** Inspect the memory/context an agent was operating with at any given point.
        *   **Error Logs with Stack Traces:** Detailed technical information for developers to debug agent failures.

### IV. Collaboration & Administration

**1. Project & Workspace Management:**
    *   **Description:** Organize agents and pipelines into logical projects or workspaces, facilitating team collaboration.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Create/Manage Projects:** Assign names, descriptions, and associate team members.
        *   **Project-scoped Resources:** Agents, pipelines, and tools can be assigned to specific projects.
        *   **Resource Sharing:** Control visibility and edit permissions within and across projects.

**2. User & Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):**
    *   **Description:** Granular control over who can do what within the dashboard and with which agents/pipelines.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Predefined Roles:** (e.g., Admin, Developer, Project Manager, Viewer, Data Scientist).
        *   **Custom Roles:** Define specific permissions (e.g., "can deploy agent," "can edit prompt," "can view logs").
        *   **User/Group Management:** Assign users to roles and manage groups for easier permission management.

**3. Version Control & Deployment Management:**
    *   **Description:** Manage different versions of agents and pipelines, and streamline their deployment to various environments.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Agent/Pipeline Versioning:** Automatically or manually create new versions of agents/pipelines after changes.
        *   **Deployment Environments:** Define development, staging, production, etc.
        *   **One-Click Deployment:** Deploy specific agent/pipeline versions to chosen environments.
        *   **Rollback Capability:** Revert to previous working versions.
        *   **Approval Workflows:** For critical deployments, require explicit approval from designated users.

**4. Audit Logs:**
    *   **Description:** A chronological record of all significant actions taken within the dashboard.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **User, Action, Timestamp:** Who did what and when.
        *   **Resource Affected:** Which agent, pipeline, or setting was modified.
        *   **Change Details:** Specifics of the modification (e.g., "Agent X prompt changed from [old] to [new]").

### V. Integrations & Extensibility

**1. API Access & SDK:**
    *   **Description:** Programmatic access to Kore functions for advanced users and external system integration.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **RESTful API:** For interacting with agents, triggering pipelines, retrieving metrics.
        *   **Python SDK:** A convenient library for developers to integrate Kore into their applications.

**2. External System Connectors:**
    *   **Description:** Seamless integration with other enterprise tools.
    *   **Sub-features:**
        *   **Data Sources:** Databases, data warehouses, streaming platforms.
        *   **Version Control Systems:** GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket for storing agent/pipeline definitions.
        *   **Monitoring & Logging Tools:** Grafana, Datadog, Splunk.
        *   **Notification Platforms:** Slack, Teams, Jira.

---

### User Role Considerations:

*   **Developers:** Will heavily use Agent Creation/Configuration, Tool/Skill Library, Prompt Engineering Studio, Pipeline Builder (code view), Traceability & Debugging, API Access & SDK, Version Control.
*   **Project Managers:** Will focus on Project & Workspace Management, Performance & Usage Metrics, Real-time Activity Feed, High-level Pipeline Builder, Audit Logs, Deployment Management.
*   **Data Scientists:** Will leverage Agent Creation (focused on data analysis/ML tasks), Tool/Skill Library (for data tools), Prompt Engineering Studio, Performance & Usage Metrics (for model efficiency), Traceability & Debugging.

This comprehensive set of features aims to provide a robust, user-friendly, and powerful dashboard for the Kore agentic framework, catering to the diverse needs of its user base.
